arguments":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport json\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2007_12.json', 'r') as f:\n    data = json.load(f)\n\n# Let's look for tables with 'CFI' or 'real' or 'percentage' price data\nfor i, el in enumerate(data['document']['elements']):\n    content = el.get('content')\n    if content and 'real' in content.lower() and 'price' in content.lower():\n        print(f'Element {i}:')\n        print(content[:2500])\n        print('---')\n\""}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_2037e929-41fe-401e-8e46-644ce1084b4f","role":"user","created":1775345118,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_ae94565396f941728869d281","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Element 82:\nAverage hourly earnings of production and other nonsupervisory workers grew by 3.8 percent over the 12 months ending in October, and recent nominal earnings gains have been among the strongest in 6 years. In real terms, earnings rose by 1.2 percent over the 12 months ended in September (latest available). That was down from gains of around 2.5 percent in the final quarter of 2006. Recent fluctuations in real wage growth reflect movements in overall inflation, largely caused by sharp swings in oil prices.\n---\nElement 142:\npercent in the third. In October, the rate averaged 6.38 percent, just above the 30-year mortgage rate in October 2006. The relatively low mortgage interest rate and the decline in existing home prices should help to make housing more affordable and keep the homeownership rate high. The National Association of Realtors' housing affordability index rose to 114.8 in September from a reading of 104.9 in June, well above the 2006 average of 106.2.\n---","annotations":{"priority":0.0}}],"structuredContent":{"stdout":"Element 82:\nAverage hourly earnings of production and other nonsupervisory workers grew by 3.8 percent over the 12 months ending in October, and recent nominal earnings gains have been among the strongest in 6 years. In real terms, earnings rose by 1.2 percent over the 12 months ended in September (latest available). That was down from gains of around 2.5 percent in the final quarter of 2006. Recent fluctuations in real wage growth reflect movements in overall inflation, largely caused by sharp swings in oil prices.\n---\nElement 142:\npercent in the third. In October, the rate averaged 6.38 percent, just above the 30-year mortgage rate in October 2006. The relatively lo