{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_4769ba1b-46ee-416e-9714-43a2265de7df","role":"user","created":1775345205,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_118bb6d26592486cb932ecdf","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Element 564 (type: section_header):\nINTRODUCTION: Market Yields\n\n---\n\nElement 565 (type: text):\nThe table in this section presents yields on Treasury marketable securities for maturities ranging from 1 month to 30 years.\n\n---\n\nElement 566 (type: text):\nTable MY-1 lists Treasury market bid yields at constant maturities for bills, notes, and bonds. These Constant Maturity Treasury rates (CMTs) are interpolated from the Treasury yield curve and published daily at Treasury's Domestic Finance web site, www.ustreas.gov/offices/domestic-finance/debt-management/interest-rate/ yield.shtml. The yield curve is fitted daily using a hermite cubic spline. For inputs, Treasury primarily uses the bid yields of the on-the-run securities (most recently auctioned Treasury securities in all maturity tranches that Treasury currently auctions) as\n\n---\n\nElement 567 (type: text):\nquoted in the secondary bond market as of approximately 3:30 p.m. each trading day. CMT yields are based on semiannual interest payments and are read at constant maturity points to develop a consistent data series.\n\n---\n\nElement 568 (type: text):\nThe quotations used by Treasury to calculate the bid yields and fit the yield curve are obtained by the Federal Reserve Bank of New York. The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System also publishes the Treasury constant maturity data series in its weekly Statistical Release H.15.\n\n---\n\nElement 569 (type: text):\nTreasury discontinued the 30-year constant maturity yield during the time period February 18, 2002, through February 8, 2006. Thus, 30-year yields for that time frame are not available.\n\n---\n\nElement 570 (type: caption):\nTABLE MY-1.—Treasury Market Bid Yields at Constant Maturities: Bills, Notes, and Bonds*\n\n---\n\nElement 571 (type: caption):\n[In percentages. Source: Office of Debt Management, Office of the Under Secretary for Domestic Finance]\n\n---\n\nElement 572 (type: table):\n<table><tr><th>Period</th><th>1-mo. (1)</th><th>3-mo. (2)</th><th>6-mo. (3)</th><th>1-yr. (4)</th><th>2-yr. (5)</th><th>3-yr. (6)</th><th>5-yr. (7)</th><th>7-yr. (8)</th><th>10-yr. (9)</th><th>20-yr. (10)</th><th>30-yr. (11)</th></tr><tr><td colspan=\"12\">Monthly average:</td></tr><tr><td>2006 - Oct.</td><td>4.97</td><td>5.05</td><td>5.12</td><td>5.01</td><td>4.80</td><td>4.72</td><td>4.69</td><td>4.69</td><td>4.73</td><td>4.94</td><td>4.85</td></tr><tr><td>Nov</td><td>5.21</td><td>5.07</td><td>5.15</td><td>5.01</td><td>4.74</td><td>4.64</td><td>4.58</td><td>4.58</td><td>4.60</td><td>4.78</td><td>4.69</td></tr><tr><td>Dec</td><td>4.87</td><td>4.97</td><td>5.07</td><td>4.94</td><td>4.67</td><td>4.58</td><td>4.53</td><td>4.54</td><td>4.56</td><td>4.78</td><td>4.68</td></tr><tr><td>2007 - Jan.</td><td>4.94</td><td>5.11</td><td>5.15</td><td>5.06</td><td>4.88</td><td>4.79</td><td>4.75</td><td>4.75</td><td>4.76</td><td>4.95</td><td>4.85</td></tr><tr><td>Feb</td><td>5.18</td><td>5.16</td><td>5.16</td><td>5.05</td><td>4.85</td><td>4.75</td><td>4.71</td><td>4.71</td><td>4.72</td><td>4.93</td><td>4.82</td></tr><tr><td>Mar</td><td>5.21</td><td>5.08</td><td>5.10</td><td>4.92</td><td>4.57</td><td>4.51</td><td>4.48</td><td>4.50</td><td>4.56</td><td>4.81</td><td>4.72</td></tr><tr><td>Apr</td><td>4.99</td><td>5.01</td><td>5.07</td><td>4.93</td><td>4.67</td><td>4.60</td><td>4.59</td><td>4.62</td><td>4.69</td><td>4.95</td><td>4.87</td></tr><tr><td>May</td><td>4.82</td><td>4.87</td><td>4.98</td>\n\n---\n\nElement 573 (type: caption):\n* Rates are from the Treasurv yields curve.\n\n---\n\nElement 574 (type: page_footer):\nDecember 2007\n\n---\n\nElement 575 (type: page_number):\n50\n\n---\n\nElement 576 (type: section_header):\nINTRODUCTION: U.S. Currency and Coin Outstanding and in Circulation\n\n---\n\nElement 577 (type: text):\nThe U.S. Currency and Coin Outstanding and in Circulation (USCC) statement informs the public of the total face value of currency and coin used as a medium of exchange that is in circulation at the end of a given accounting month. The statement defines the total amount of currency and coin outstanding and the portion deemed to be in circulation. It includes some old and current rare issues that do not circulate or that may do so to a limited extent. Treasury includes them in the statement because the issues were originally intended for general circulation.\n\n---\n\nElement 578 (type: text):\nThe USCC statement provides a description of the various issues of paper money. It also gives an estimated average of currency and coin held by each individual, using estimates of population from the Bureau of the Census. USCC information has been published by Treasury since 1888, and was published separately until 1983, when it was incorporated into the \"Treasury Bulletin.\" The USCC comes from monthly reports compiled by Treasury offices, U.S. Mint offices, the Federal Reserve banks (FRBs), and the Federal Reserve Board.\n\n---\n\nElement 579 (type: section_header):\nTABLE USCC-1.—Amounts Outstanding and in Circulation, September 30, 2007\n\n---\n\nElement 580 (type: text):\n[Source: Financial Management Service]\n\n---\n\nElement 581 (type: table):\n<table><tr><th>Currency</th><th>Total currency and coin (1)</th><th>Total currency (2)</th><th>Federal Reserve notes 1 (3)</th><th>U.S. notes (4)</th><th>Currency no longer issued (5)</th></tr><tr><td>Amounts outstanding</td><td>$1,039,297,466,081</td><td>$1,001,154,872,178</td><td>$1,000,658,449,847</td><td>$250,265,766</td><td>$246,156,565</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"6\">Less amounts held by:</td></tr><tr><td>The Treasury</td><td>361,168,573</td><td>26,107,029</td><td>25,889,553</td><td>7,505</td><td>209,971</td></tr><tr><td>FRBs</td><td>228,358,787,580</td><td>227,2